<article class="env-definition" id="0285">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0285">Definition <span data-tag="0285">111.28.1</span>.</a> A Noetherian local ring $A$ is said to be <em>Cohen-Macaulay</em> of dimension $d$ if it has dimension $d$ and there exists a system of parameters $x_1, \ldots , x_ d$ for $A$ such that $x_ i$ is a nonzerodivisor in $A/(x_1, \ldots , x_{i-1})$ for $i = 1, \ldots , d$. </p>
</article>